2. DESCRIPTION OF THE RESEARCH PROJECT

Urban GIST - Green Infrastructure for Stress reduction and Thermal comfort:

2.1 Goals of the research

The project focuses on the exploration of the role Urban Green Infrastructure (UGI) design plays
in citizen well-being, with a focus on Thermal Comfort (TC) and Stress Level Reduction
(SLR). While the positive role of nature on general well-being is widely recognized, the UGI
implementation characteristics that most effectively contribute to the improvement of TC and SLR
are yet to be identified. To address this research gap, this thesis will implement a novel
pedestrian-centered research methodology combining environmental and biometric sensing, eye-
tracking and immersive media technologies, and participant surveys, through the deployment of
off-site and on-site experimental campaigns.

Prior literature that has focused on ‘macro’ or city scale studies, have highlighted the beneficial
role of Nature-based Solutions (NbS) for heat island, noise or air pollution mitigation (Johnson et
al. 2020; Llaguno-Munitxa and Bou-Zeid 2020a; Manoli et al. 2019). Making use of hybrid physics
and data-driven models, urban fabric, microclimate and air quality, health reports, and socio-
demographic metrics have been studied, and the positive role of NbS for urban health have also
been reported (Sowińska-Świerkosz et al. 2021). At the ‘micro’ or neighborhood scale, local
sensing approaches using carry-on sensing kits have enabled the study of the local environmental
gradients to high spatiotemporal resolutions (Apte et al. 2017; Pigliautile and Pisello 2018) and
have reported the benefits of local UGI to lower air temperature and enhancement of thermal
comfort. Publications focusing on the study of the Physiological Equivalent Temperature (PET) -
the complete heat budget of the human body- have combined biometric and environmental
sensing approaches aiming to obtain quantifiable metrics of urban thermal comfort (Villadiego
and Dabat 2014; Piselli et al. 2018). However, despite the extensive literature, research focused
on i) the study the role small to medium scale UGI plays in thermal and psychological stress, and
the ii) study of local UGI characteristics that are most effective to enhance thermal comfort and
SLR are yet to be developed.

With the aim to address these research gaps, this thesis will focus on the development of a
pedestrian-centered research methodology where objective metrics on the UGI infrastructure
quantification (computed using datasets collected through LiDAR scanning, remote sensing and
GIS databases), urban microclimate data (collected through a carry on sensing kit containing
sensors for temperature, relative humidity, and solar radiation amongst others), and biometric
data (collected through immersive media, eye-tracking, and wearable sensing technologies), will
be combined with subjective metrics acquired through pedestrian survey campaigns. That is, a
pedestrian-centered research methodology is proposed whereby not only environmental and
physiological metrics, but also psychological aspects will be evaluated to identify small to medium
scale UGI and their characteristics that most effectively contribute towards thermal comfort and
stress level reduction.

The thesis will address the following research questions:
   i) What is the role small to medium scale urban GI implementations and their characteristics -
       ratio, geometry, type, forest biodiversity- play in thermal comfort and stress level reduction?
   ii) Can we through the deployment of a pedestrian-centered research methodology better
       report the pedestrian level multi-domain perception associated to thermal comfort?
   iii) Are environmental stressors and especially so thermal stress a main driver of psychological
       stress?

