2.2 State of the art

One third of adult’s report suffering from high physiological stress levels, and these numbers are
escalating, driven by global trends towards urbanization and increased socioeconomic and
environmental pressures such as those recently induced by the COVID-19 pandemic (Cole et al.
2020; Sharifi and Khavarian-Garmsir 2020). Similarly, the incidence of reported urban heat stress
cases is expected to worsen with global warming and urban densification, leading to synergies
between Urban Heat Island (UHI) and heat waves which can cause mortality and severe heat-
related illnesses. UHI phenomena is caused by the urban morphology and impervious materials
-with thermal properties different to those of green areas- which lead to the modification of the
energy budget and the evapotranspiration potential affecting both air UHI and surface UHI and
causing thermal discomfort (Manoli et al. 2019; Founda and Santamouris 2017). Furthermore,
environmental stressors such as increased temperatures have also been identified as critical
drivers of psychological stress (Tawatsupa et al. 2012). The beneficial role of UGI for citizen
comfort is undeniable. The possibilities of nature-based restoration approaches for SLR are
already being discussed (Orchard and Schiel 2021), and UGI implementations have already
proved to be very beneficial to lower air temperatures up to 6°C (Jayasooriya et al. 2017; Imran
et al. 2019). However, prior studies have not yet focused on the study of the role small to medium
scale UGI implementations and their spatial characteristics, also called ‘nature quality’, play in
pedestrian level comfort. The presence of GI however in cities is very heterogeneous, and so is
the case in the Brussels Capital Region (BCR) (Van de Voorde 2017). Urban GI implementations
vary in -ratio, geometry, type (trees, bushes, green patches), and forest biodiversity.

Through the increasing accessibility to mobile environmental sensing technologies, pedestrian
level environmental sensing campaigns are becoming progressively more popular contributing to
the understanding of the strong spatial environmental gradients we are exposed to in cities, with
potentially up to 8 times higher scalar concentrations within an urban block distance (Apte et al.
2017). However, while such studies are starting to report reductions in air temperature and
improvements in urban comfort to be associated to the local presence of UGI, high spatial
resolution direct sensing campaigns are yet limited, and further studies are required to
characterize urban environmental gradients to high spatio-temporal resolutions as they relate to
the local UGI characteristics. On the other hand, through the increasing access to Extended
Reality (XR), eye tracking, and wearable biometric sensing technologies, the study of nature and
urban scene preferences and their impact on psychological stress levels or overall comfort, are
starting to be studied (Liszio and Masuch 2019; Lu et al. 2020). However, prior literature that has
made use of XR, and biometric sensing technologies, have generally focused on the study of
dichotomic immersion comparisons -such as green vs not green or urban vs rural-. Furthermore,
amongst them, the studies that evaluated the restorative effect of nature for SLR for example,
solely focused on laboratory - off-site experiments. However, these studies have already shown
the benefits of combining XR and eye-tracking technologies, with biometric sensing and
subjective participant feedback questionnaires, to evaluate the multi-domain perception and
impact of the visualized scenes on the participant stress levels. The research methodologies to
aim for a holistic study for the evaluation of thermal comfort and stress are yet to be further
defined, however prior studies that have made use of the above-mentioned methodologies, have
proved efficient to capture pedestrian level microclimate gradients at high spatial resolutions, and
to combine biometric and eye tracking datasets with participant feedback questionnaires to reveal
heat stress exposure indicators, or visual exploration patterns and heart rate variability associated
to high stress levels.

2.3 Research project

This thesis is based on the working hypothesis that not only large scale, but also small to medium
scale UGI that is integrated within the urban fabric, will modulate pedestrian level comfort (thermal
comfort and stress particularly). Thus, it will be the objective of this research to identify the role

                      5
                                                                             F.R.S.-FNRS GRANTS AND FELLOWSHIPS CALL 2022
                                                                                                            'Research Fellow' (ASP - Aspirant)

distinct GI characteristics (e.g., the ratio, geometry, type - trees, bushes, green patches, forest
biodiversity) play in the enhancement of TC and SLR.

The second working hypothesis is that there will be significant correlations between SLR and
PET. Thus, the objective will be to highlight the role environmental stressors, such as the
exposure to increased temperatures, play in urban comfort, at physiological and psychological
levels.

Designing an urban square to promote thermal comfort, emotional wellness, and reduce
physiological stress levels is a challenging task given that pedestrian level physiological and
psychological reactions are triggered by numerous environmental factors. The third working
hypothesis of this research is that for the study of thermal comfort and stress, it is necessary to
adopt a multi-domain (including visual, thermal and acoustic parameters) and pedestrian-
centered research methodology. This research will not only include quantitative metrics, but also
subjective measures to account for the participant’s perception of environmental, physiological
and psychological dimensions of urban comfort.

To our knowledge no comprehensive study focusing on urban comfort evaluation and particularly
thermal comfort and stress has been developed to date where i) microclimate sensing, ii) high
spatial resolution urban GI documentation, iii) biometric and eye-tracking measurements, and iii)
subjective participant assessment, have been combined (see Fig.1). This research will build on
the work developed through the one-year long Louvain4City grant obtained in 2021 “Brussels
Street Greenery and Citizen Comfort Evaluation” by Prof. Llaguno-Munitxa (promoter) and
developed in collaboration with Prof. Edwards (co-promoter), where virtual reality and biometric
sensing based ‘off-site’ -or in laboratory- participant testing protocols were explored (see Fig. 2 &
3). This research will further develop the UGI quantification and participant testing strategies
proposed for the Louvain4City grant, and will aim at the formulation of not only off-site but also
on-site urban participant testing protocols.

With the ambition to test this working methodology, the research will start with a systematic review
and city level UGI analysis, and will follow with the preparation of the experimental campaigns.
The final year of the research will be focused to the data analysis and finalization of the thesis
writing.

2.4 Work plan

The thesis will be organized in 4 main stages that will be developed during the 4 years of the
proposed research. The research will adopt the Brussels Capital Region (BCR) as the case study.

1) Systematic Review (4 months):

A systematic review on urban GI for improved PET and SLR will be developed. This will form the
first paper of the PhD. At the same time, we will apply for an ethics agreement for human
participants testing.

2) UGI documentation: (8 months):

Remote sensing data to compute the Land Surface Temperature (LST) and the Normalized
Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), and UrBIS LiDAR aerial point cloud datasets will be used to
quantify the UGI in BCR squares. For a selection of squares, a high spatial-resolution pedestrian
level UGI characterization through segmentation analysis and semantic classification – using a
LiDAR Faro-M70 scanner and an Insta-Pro 360° camera (available in LOCI-MiiL) - will be
developed. The proposed GI quantification methodology will be published as a technical paper.
Aside from the UGI quantification and characterization, the collected 360° videos and 3D scans,

                                                               6
                                                                             F.R.S.-FNRS GRANTS AND FELLOWSHIPS CALL 2022
                                                                                                            'Research Fellow' (ASP - Aspirant)

will enable the projection of the recorded urban square scenes in a VR environment for the
development of the off-site experimental campaign as per phase 4.1.

3) Experimental campaign preparation; (12 months)

The PET will be computed using a sensing-kit including: sound, temperature, relative-humidity,
wind speed, wind direction, barometric pressure, and solar radiation sensors. Wearable sensors
for Heart Rate Variability (HRV) and Skin Conductance (SC) will be used to identify the Stress
Level Reduction (SLR) (Dalmeida and Masala 2021; Edwards et al. 2015). Through the XR
headsets -VivePro HTC and Pupil core-, eye-tracking data will be collected - gaze, pupil diameter,
blinks and head position-, and visual fixations and Areas of Interest (AoI) will be computed. These
metrics will be used as indicators to identify UGI interest zones, and associate them with PET
reduction and SLR. Participant feedback questionnaires such as the state Trait Anxiety Inventory
(STAI) (Spielberger 1983), the Positive and Negative Affect Schedule (PANAS) (Watson et al.
1988), the Perceived Restorativeness Scale (PRS) (Hartig et al. 1997), and Connectedness to
Nature Scale (CNS) (Navarro et al. 2017) will be included in the experimental campaign. The
biometric and survey variables will be studied as the dependent variables and the root mean
square of the successive differences and ANOVA for the repeated measures will be studied for
the selected squares with distinct UGI characteristics. A methods paper describing the planning
of the experimental campaigns will be published.

4) Experimental campaigns:
Following the approval of the ethics committee, the experimental campaigns will be initiated by
by an intensive participant recruiting process.

4.1. Experimental campaign Off-site (8 months):
The participants will be tested in the XR OpenHub in LLN using biometric sensors for HRV and
SC, and feedback questionnaires to report the SLR. Using the 360° videos and scans captured
during phase 2, the urban scenes will be visualized in a VR headset - VivePro HTC, and through
its implemented eye-tracking technologies, the gaze behavior will be analyzed to identify the AoI
and fixations (Edwards et al. 2003). The sounds of the recorded urban scenes will also be
preserved, and situations where very intrusive objects dominate the urban scene will be avoided.
The questionnaires will need to be filled in prior, during and after the experiment. The off-site
protocol will enable a controlled environment to identify the percentage of time spent in the UGI
AoI and fixations, and their relation with SLR. A paper describing the off-site experimental protocol
and the research findings will be prepared.

4.2. Experimental campaign On-site (8 months):
The recruited participants will be transported to the selected squares in BCR (selection of ~12
squares made in phase 2), where they will carry a wearable sensing kit including the microclimate
and biometric sensors (see list in 3) to measure the PET reduction and SLR associated to the
quantified UGI characteristics (as per phase 2). The Pupil AR headset will be used to study the
on-site UGI fixations and AoI. On-site pedestrian surveys will also be filled in. Through regression
analysis, the UGI metrics and PET reduction, PET and SLR, and off-site and on-site
measurements will be studied. The experimental campaign will be developed two times per day
for all squares. Days with clear weather conditions, and with low urban occupancy will be selected
for all studied sites. A paper describing the on-site experimental protocol and the research findings
will be prepared.

Finally, in the last 8 months of thesis, the final data analysis protocols, and the comparison
between the tested participant testing approaches will be evaluated. The papers initiated during
the phases 4.1 and 4.2 will be concluded. The last months of the research will be dedicated to
the thesis formatting and deposition.

