4. POTENTIAL INTERDISCIPLINARY APPROACH OF THE RESEARCH PROJECT
   (OPTIONAL)

The proposed research focuses on the study of the role urban green infrastructure characteristics
play on urban comfort - stress level reduction and thermal comfort in particular. This research
requires the development of quantitative and qualitative, interdisciplinary research methodologies
that combine: urban science, environmental science, experimental psychology and cognitive
neuroscience perspectives.

         My research experience is focused on the study of environmental sciences (focusing on
thermal comfort and environmental sustainability), resource allocation, cloud computing and
landscape planning, classification and prediction maps for forecasted years, data fusion research
and multi-criteria decision analysis. Given the interdisciplinarity of the research proposal, my
research expertise will be complemented with the environmental and urban science background
of promoter Prof. Llaguno-Munitxa from UCLouvain - Louvain Landscape, Architecture and Built
(LAB) institute, and with the expertise on experimental psychology and cognitive neuroscience
provided by the co-promoter, Prof Edwards president of the UCLouvain Psychological Sciences
Research Institute (IPSY). An international collaboration with Prof. Manoli from the University
College London - Department of Civil, Environmental & Geomatic Engineering has also been
established. Prof. Manoli is specialized in environmental sciences and urban forestry.

         My research background will be complemented by that of Prof. Llaguno-Munitxa and the
research group, Architecture et Climat group of LAB at UCLouvain. The Architecture et Climat
group fundamental and applied research interests include, amongst others, the study of urban
science, climate and health as well as the study of environmental quality and human comfort
factors. The urban analysis workflows for the quantification and characterization of the urban
green infrastructure, and the environmental sensing campaign preparation (sensor testing and
calibration), will be developed within the Architecture et Climat lab and under the supervision of
Prof. Llaguno-Munitxa. The expertise of Prof. Manoli and his collaborators from the Urban
Ecohydrology Lab at the University College London, will also be instrumental to the development
of the sensing protocol. Prof Manoli will be joining the School of Architecture, Civil and
Environmental Engineering at EPFL as professor in September 2022, where he will be hosting
me in his lab during a stay of 90 days during the phase 3 of the research.

         The preparation of the biometric sensing protocol and participant questionnaire
methodology and testing will be developed in collaboration with (co-promoter) Prof. Edwards and
his collaborators from the Psy-NAPS lab from IPSY- UCLouvain, (associated with the Institute of
Neuroscience and Louvain Bionics). The contribution of Prof. Edwards will focus on the guidance
of the VR experimental approaches, the biometric sensing protocol and the use of participant
questionnaire methodology, as well as providing support equipment from his Psy-NAPS group.

    The research that I will be working on, will benefit from the interdisciplinary project developed
as part of the grant Louvain4City 2021 “Brussels Street Greenery and Citizen Comfort Evaluation”
PI- Asst Prof. Llaguno-Munitxa. Within UCLouvain, this work was performed in collaboration with
Prof. Edwards (president of IPSY at UCLouvain) and the Media Innovation & Intelligibility Lab
(MiiL) through the contribution of Prof. Sebastien Nahon, as well as through the collaboration with
the OpenHub XR team led by Dr. Sonneville. Aside from the internal UCLouvain collaborations,
the Louvain4City grant initiated an international collaboration I would like to build on with Prof.
Tzyy Ping, co-Director of the Center for Advanced Neurological Engineering, Institute for Neural
Computation (INC) and Institute of Engineering in Medicine (IEM), in the University of California,
San Diego. Furthermore, a collaboration with distinguished Prof. Eduardo Macagno from the
department of Developmental Biology at UCSanDiego and specialized in the novel
interdisciplinary area: Architecture and Neuroscience was established. Prof. Eduardo Macagno
is also co-Director of the Academy of Neuroscience for Architecture.

