5. DESCRIPTION OF THE WORK ENVIRONMENT

The promoter Prof. Llaguno-Munitxa is a professor at UCLouvain where she is a member of the
faculty LOCI (Architecture, Architectural Engineering, Urbanism), and the Louvain Landscape,
Architecture and Built environment (LAB) research institute (created in January 2021 to sustain,
coordinate, nurture and manage all research activities at UCLouvain in the fields of architecture,
architectural engineering, urban and landscape design, and territorial development). Within LAB,
the research group Prof. Llaguno-Munitxa is part of, the Architecture et Climat group - founded in
1980, is one of the most recognized and longest-standing entities within UCLouvain.

         As PhD candidate, I will be part of the Louvain research Institute LAB and within it, the
Architecture et Climat research group. The group focuses its fundamental and applied research
interests on four interrelated areas of 1) urban science, climate and health; 2) life cycle
performance and parametric modelling at urban and building scale; 3) building performance
simulation and energy flows predictions 4) environmental quality and human factors. The
research of Prof Maider Llaguno-Munitxa is focused on the first pilar of research within the group,
that is the study of urban science, comfort and health. The research methodologies she uses in
her work include direct sensing, environmental modeling, and data-driven approaches. Working
alongside several national and international partners, the architecture et climat group has
developed significant expertise in the development of energy performance simulation modelling
and analysis of dynamic climate-based phenomena including the thermal comfort and ventilation.
The group regularly publishes papers in peer-reviewed international journals and is invited at
national and international conferences. Within Architecture et Climat, over 30 PhD theses have
been completed since its creation, including several FNRS-funded scholarships. Furthermore,
through the collaboration with the co-promoter Prof. Edwards, and his lab members at Psy-NAPS,
I will engage with biometric sensing technologies for the heart rate variability, skin conductance
as well as the potential usage of Electroencephalography. This collaboration will also be critical
for the analysis and rationalization of the collected datasets, and development of the participant
survey protocols. My stay during 3 months at the EPFL lab of Prof. Manoli (currently at the
University College London) will enable the further testing and evaluation of the microclimate
sensing protocol and the computation of the Perceived Equivalent Temperature (PET).

