6. SUMMARY OF THE MASTER’S THESIS OR EQUIVALENT

Urban Heat Environment is subjective to various phenological & anthropogenic changes
throughout the day. However, satellites have always faced gain and loss bias towards prioritizing
spatial resolution vs temporal resolution, which has accounted for various time gaps between
consecutive observations. Gaining higher spatial resolution would require satellite to be placed in
near-earth orbit, which takes longer time for instrument to circle back around the world to initial
position for a secondary observation. Additionally, detailed observations require higher
radiometric capability, which requires more ground controls to receive data, which makes the
entire enterprise expensive. And near-earth satellites have historically used extensive fuel to
maintain orbital position, resulting in a lower lifespan. Such problems prevent satellite data to give
a complete overview of the Earth phenomenon, and to compensate for such gaps in satellite
sensing, ground sensors were developed to sense any desired parameter and store locally or
transmit data under private radio frequencies. Such sensing is referred to as hyper-local sensing
and accounts for sensing phenomena irrespective of daylight and can help fill voids in satellite
sensing.

But as the sensors act as a proxy of satellite sensing, the location of placing the sensor has been
of great debate especially since the exact location to place sensor is subjective to individual
observers. A standard approach, if it were to be developed, would require a holistic observation
of the entire region to pinpoint location based on environment.

The use of this study will provide an aggregated framework for carrying out sensor placement,
which will be focused on the phenomenon being perceived and unbiased from human perception
and will be more efficient technologically.

Spatiotemporal data fusion was useful in prioritizing the grid cells that have more influence to the
urban heat gains, and sensor placement strategy which was based on these priorities were
carried out. Further, the varieties of existing LCZ within grids were considered and estimate of the
overall sensors required for monitoring these features were mathematically established. And
further the authorities that have a set budget for purchasing the sensors, can use the proposed
method for understanding the minimum number of sensors needed for a city-wide sensing and
along with locations that can be prioritized.

This prioritization scheme was purely dependent on the measured heat intensities over surfaces
and the network source (LoRa Gateway) placement was based on the maximum coverage
potential under the terrain considerations of study area, and thereby optimization of network
sources was also carried out. These network serves have to be placed in secure locations, and
therefore the government buildings and public pre-existing infrastructure was mapped with OSM
data, where sufficient range can be achieved for end sensor placement.

Finally with the established variability at the end device, gateway, terrain consideration and public
infrastructure availability, the sensor placement map was proposed which would be optimal and
unbiased towards the Local Climate Zones being studied, and would be based on the
phenomenon of Urban Heat being sensed.

